<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!-- Sprint 41 Track C (#380): the round trip that lets the words the app
       says be edited as WRITING rather than as code.

       WHY THIS EXISTS. The Fixer's operator-facing prose lives as inline C#
       string literals, wrapped across lines and joined with '+'. Tuning three
       words in the middle of that means finding the right literal, minding the
       escaping, minding the wrap, and never seeing the sentence the operator
       will actually hear. For an editor working with a screen reader, every one
       of those is friction paid on every edit, and there are hundreds of
       strings.

       WHAT IT DOES. `extract` reads the source and writes one markdown file
       whose headings are the strings and whose paragraphs are the ASSEMBLED
       sentences — a stem plus a suffix plus a fragment appears as the one
       sentence it becomes, with runtime values shown filled in. `apply` reads
       that file back and writes only the sentences that changed, into the
       exact literals they came from.

       NOT part of the app build or the solution — same footing as
       tools/refvoice. It is run by hand and its output is committed.

       Run:  tools\prose\prose extract
             tools\prose\prose read
             tools\prose\prose check
             tools\prose\prose apply

       ROSLYN, NOT REGEX, ON PURPOSE. The strings this walks are concatenations
       split across lines, some of them inside fluent StringBuilder chains with
       live values spliced between the halves. That is precisely the shape a
       text scan gets wrong, and getting it wrong here means writing malformed
       C# into a file the operator's radio control depends on. The syntax tree
       gives exact spans, so a rewrite touches the literal and nothing else, and
       the result is re-parsed before a byte reaches disk. Radios.Tests already
       takes this package for the same reason (FieldKeyMapScan). -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AssemblyName>prose</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Prose</RootNamespace>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" Version="4.14.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="surfaces\**" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <!-- The suite is its own project; without this the SDK's **/*.cs glob
         compiles it into the tool. -->
    <Compile Remove="Prose.Tests\**" />
    <None Remove="Prose.Tests\**" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
